<div class="eng"><div class="lab">GESHE MICHAEL'S ENGLISH (VERBATIM)</div>
<span class="u" data-d="1" data-l="s247S">Here we will <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w6">analyze</span> the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, &quot;...The <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w4">mind</span> of a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w2">craving spirit</span> <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s247p5">as well</span>, which sees a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w1">stream of water</span> as <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w3">pus</span>.&quot; One may begin with the following question: Let&#x27;s consider the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w27">objects</span> of the following states of mind: the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w13">visual consciousness</span> of a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w12">craving spirit</span> where a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w7">river of water</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w11">looks like</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w8"><span class="u" data-d="7" data-l="s247m9">pus</span> and <span class="u" data-d="7" data-l="s247m10">blood</span></span>; the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w20">visual consciousness</span> of a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w18">person with a kind of <span class="u" data-d="7" data-l="s247m19">cataract</span></span> where a clean white <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w14">porcelain basin</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w17">looks like</span> a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w15">hair</span> has <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w16">fallen</span> into it; and that kind of <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w26">meditation</span> where you <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w25">visualize</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w24">skeletons</span>--where you imagine that the entire <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w21">surface of the earth</span> is <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w23">covered</span> with the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w22">bones of corpses</span>. Are all these objects <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s247w29">completely</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s247w30">equivalent</span>, as far as being something that <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s247w28">exists or doesn&#x27;t exist</span>?</span>
</div>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>AI NOTE</b> &mdash; PROVISIONAL machine alignment by Codex (Tibetan-first independent proposal); no semantic approval implied. The verse and each of the three examples remain distinct. The two keng rus occurrences cross in English: first = bones of corpses; second = skeletons. bse ru = porcelain basin and rab rib can = person with a kind of cataract follow this source and current HGM lexical evidence; clean white is supplied. yod med and rnam pa kun du are phrase alignments. Omitted: distributed quotation/question framing, interior-location recast, the third snang ba'i visualization frame, and the collapsed second mi mtshungs alternative; omission is not a null judgment. Full original source text is retained.</div>
